%% chapter-51.ltx — Type-Directed Compilation
%%

\label{ch:type-directed}

The compilation of typed languages raises a fundamental question: what happens to types at runtime? The answer determines the compilation strategy, the runtime representation of data, and the performance characteristics of the generated code. This chapter explores the spectrum from type erasure to type passing, the compilation of type classes via dictionary passing, and the generation of type-specialized code.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 51.1
%% ============================================================
\section{Type Erasure Semantics}\label{sec:type-erasure}

In the type erasure approach, all type information is removed during compilation. The generated code contains no type annotations, no type checks, and no type abstractions.

\subsection{Erasure for Parametric Polymorphism}\label{sec:erasure-parametric}

For parametrically polymorphic programs, erasure is straightforward: type abstractions become the identity, and type applications are removed.

\begin{example}[Erasing System F]\label{ex:erase-system-f}
The System~F term:
\[
  \Lambda \alpha.\, \lambda x : \alpha.\, x
\]
erases to the untyped term:
\[
  \lambda x.\, x
\]
The type abstraction and the type annotation on $x$ are removed. The resulting code works for all types because it does not inspect or manipulate the type of $x$.
\end{example}

\subsection{Erasure Theorem}\label{sec:erasure-theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Type Erasure Preserves Semantics]\label{thm:erasure}
If $\vdash e : \tau$ in System~F and $e \longrightarrow e'$, then $\lfloor e \rfloor \longrightarrow \lfloor e' \rfloor$ in the untyped lambda calculus, where $\lfloor \cdot \rfloor$ is the erasure function.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By induction on the reduction relation. The key cases are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Beta-reduction: $\lfloor (\lambda x : \tau.\, e)\; v \rfloor = (\lambda x.\, \lfloor e \rfloor)\; \lfloor v \rfloor \longrightarrow \lfloor e \rfloor [\lfloor v \rfloor / x] = \lfloor e[v/x] \rfloor$.
  \item Type-beta: $\lfloor (\Lambda \alpha.\, e)\; [\tau] \rfloor = \lfloor e \rfloor \longrightarrow \lfloor e \rfloor$. The type application erases to nothing.
\end{itemize}
\qed
\end{proof}

\subsection{Erasure for Data Types}\label{sec:erasure-data}

Algebraic data types are erased to tagged unions or boxed representations. For example, the type $\mathrm{List}\; \alpha$ erases to a linked list of boxed values, where each node contains a tag (cons or nil) and pointers to the head and tail.

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Source:
  data List a = Nil | Cons a (List a)

  -- Erased representation:
  data List = Nil | Cons Box (Ptr List)
\end{verbatim}

