\section{Type-Passing Semantics}\label{sec:type-passing}

In the type-passing approach, some or all type information is retained at runtime. This enables runtime type tests, type-case analysis, and type-specialized dispatch.

\subsection{Runtime Type Information}\label{sec:rtti}

Runtime type information (RTTI) allows the program to inspect the type of a value at runtime:

\begin{verbatim}
  if (x instanceof String) {
    String s = (String) x;
    // use s as a String
  }
\end{verbatim}

RTTI is essential for:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Dynamic casting.} Converting a value of a supertype to a subtype.
  \item \textbf{Pattern matching.} Dispatching on the runtime type of a value.
  \item \textbf{Reflection.} Inspecting and manipulating types at runtime.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Type-Passing for Generics}\label{sec:type-passing-generics}

In Java, generics are erased (like in OCaml), but some type information is retained for reflection. In C\#, generics are fully reified: each type instantiation generates a distinct runtime type.

\begin{example}[C\# generics]\label{ex:csharp-generics}
\begin{verbatim}
  List<int> ints = new List<int>();
  List<string> strings = new List<string>();
  // ints.GetType() != strings.GetType()
  // The runtime maintains distinct types for List<int> and List<string>
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Monomorphization}\label{sec:monomorphization}

Monomorphization generates a specialized copy of a polymorphic function for each type it is instantiated with:

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Source:
  fn add<T: Add>(x: T, y: T) -> T { x + y }

  -- Monomorphized:
  fn add_i32(x: i32, y: i32) -> i32 { x + y }
  fn add_f64(x: f64, y: f64) -> f64 { x + y }
\end{verbatim}

Monomorphization eliminates all overhead of polymorphism at the cost of code bloat. It is used by Rust and C++ templates.

