\section{Dict-Passing for Type Classes}\label{sec:dict-passing}

The standard compilation strategy for type classes is \emph{dictionary passing}: each type class constraint is compiled as an additional parameter (a dictionary) containing the implementations of the class methods.

\subsection{Dictionary Construction}\label{sec:dict-construction}

\begin{example}[Compiling a type class]\label{ex:compile-typeclass}
\begin{verbatim}
  -- Source (Haskell):
  class Eq a where
    (==) :: a -> a -> Bool

  instance Eq Int where
    x == y = primEqInt x y

  member :: Eq a => a -> [a] -> Bool
  member x [] = False
  member x (y:ys) = x == y || member x ys

  -- Compiled (System F with dictionaries):
  data EqDict a = EqDict { eq :: a -> a -> Bool }

  eqInt :: EqDict Int
  eqInt = EqDict { eq = primEqInt }

  member :: forall a. EqDict a -> a -> [a] -> Bool
  member dict x [] = False
  member dict x (y:ys) = (eq dict) x y || member dict x ys
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Dictionary Specialization}\label{sec:dict-specialization}

When a polymorphic function is called at a known type, the dictionary can be specialized (inlined), eliminating the overhead of dictionary lookup:

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Before specialization:
  member eqInt 3 [1, 2, 3]

  -- After specialization (inlining the dictionary):
  member_spec 3 [1, 2, 3]
  where member_spec x [] = False
        member_spec x (y:ys) = primEqInt x y || member_spec x ys
\end{verbatim}

