\section{Type-Specialized Code Generation}\label{sec:type-specialized}

Type-specialized code generation produces different code for different type instantiations, optimizing for the specific types involved.

\subsection{Specialization for Primitive Types}\label{sec:specialization-primitive}

When a polymorphic function is instantiated with a primitive type, the compiler can generate specialized code that uses primitive operations:

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Generic:
  fn swap<T>(x: &mut T, y: &mut T) { let tmp = *x; *x = *y; *y = tmp; }

  -- Specialized for i32:
  fn swap_i32(x: &mut i32, y: &mut i32) {
    let tmp: i32 = *x;  // uses i32 load
    *x = *y;             // uses i32 store
    *y = tmp;
  }
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Boxed vs. Unboxed Representations}\label{sec:boxed-unboxed}

Type specialization enables the use of unboxed representations for primitive types:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Boxed:} All values are pointers to heap-allocated objects. Uniform representation but requires allocation and indirection.
  \item \textbf{Unboxed:} Primitive values are stored directly (e.g., 32-bit integers as 32-bit words). Efficient but requires type-specific code.
\end{itemize}

GHC uses a hybrid approach: polymorphic code uses boxed representations, but specialized code (produced by the specializer) uses unboxed representations for primitive types.

