\section{Examples: Compiling Generics, GADTs}\label{sec:compiling-examples}

\subsection{Compiling Java Generics}\label{sec:compiling-java}

Java generics use \emph{erasure} with \emph{bridges}:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Type parameters are erased to their bounds (or \texttt{Object}).
  \item Bridge methods are generated to maintain polymorphism in the presence of overriding.
  \item Casts are inserted at type parameter use sites to ensure type safety.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Source:
  class Box<T> { T value; T get() { return value; } }
  class IntBox extends Box<Integer> {
    @Override Integer get() { return super.get(); }
  }

  // Compiled (erased):
  class Box { Object value; Object get() { return value; } }
  class IntBox extends Box {
    Integer get() { return (Integer) super.get(); }  // bridge
    // synthetic bridge: Object get() { return get(); }
  }
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Compiling GADTs}\label{sec:compiling-gadts}

Generalized Algebraic Data Types (GADTs) require careful compilation because different constructors may produce different type indices.

\begin{example}[Compiling a GADT]\label{ex:compile-gadt}
\begin{verbatim}
  -- Source (Haskell):
  data Expr a where
    Lit  :: Int -> Expr Int
    Succ :: Expr Int -> Expr Int
    IsZ  :: Expr Int -> Expr Bool

  eval :: Expr a -> a
  eval (Lit n) = n
  eval (Succ e) = eval e + 1
  eval (IsZ e) = eval e == 0

  -- Compiled:
  -- Expr is represented as a tagged union
  -- eval uses type-specific branches
  -- The GADT indices are erased, but pattern matching
  -- ensures type safety at compile time
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\begin{historical}
The compilation of typed languages has evolved through several paradigms. The type erasure approach, used by ML and early Haskell, treats types as purely static. The dictionary-passing approach, developed for Haskell's type classes~\cite{wadler1989}, extends erasure with runtime dictionaries. The monomorphization approach, used by C++ templates and Rust, generates specialized code for each type instantiation. The development of type-specialized compilation in GHC~\cite{peytonjones1993} demonstrated that aggressive specialization can recover the performance of hand-specialized code while retaining the abstraction of parametric polymorphism.
\end{historical}

