\section{Advanced Coercion-Based Compilation}\label{sec:coercion-advanced}

\subsection{Coercion Semantics}\label{sec:coercion-semantics}

A \emph{coercion} is a function that converts a value from one type to another. In coercion-based compilation, subtyping is realized by inserting coercions at compile time.

\begin{definition}[Coercion]\label{def:coercion}
A \emph{coercion} from type $\tau_1$ to type $\tau_2$ is a function $c : \tau_1 \to \tau_2$ such that the semantics of the program is preserved. The coercion is inserted by the compiler at subtyping points.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Coercion for numeric widening]\label{ex:numeric-widening}
\begin{verbatim}
  // Source:
  let x : float = 42  // int <: float

  // Compiled:
  let x : float = int_to_float(42)
  // The coercion int_to_float converts 32-bit int to 64-bit float
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Coercion Composition}\label{sec:coercion-composition}

When subtyping involves multiple steps, the compiler composes the coercions:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Source: Int <: Num <: Eq (transitive subtyping)
  let x : Eq = 42

  // Compiled:
  let x : Eq = coerce_Num_to_Eq(coerce_Int_to_Num(42))
  // = coerce_Int_to_Eq(42)  (after composition)
\end{verbatim}

\begin{theorem}[Coercion Composition]\label{thm:coercion-composition}
If $c_1 : \tau_1 \to \tau_2$ and $c_2 : \tau_2 \to \tau_3$ are coercions, then $c_2 \circ c_1 : \tau_1 \to \tau_3$ is a coercion.
\end{theorem}

The compiler can optimize coercion chains by composing them into a single coercion, eliminating intermediate conversions.

\subsection{Coercions for Type Classes}\label{sec:coercion-typeclasses}

When type classes are compiled by dictionary passing, the coercions are dictionary constructions:

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Source:
  class Show a where show : a -> String
  instance Show Int where show = primShowInt

  let x : Show = (42 : Int)  // Int has a Show instance

  -- Compiled:
  let x : Show = ShowDict { show = primShowInt }
  -- The coercion constructs a dictionary from the instance
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Eta-Expansion for Coercions}\label{sec:eta-expansion}

When a function is coerced to a subtype, the compiler may need to eta-expand it:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Source:
  let f : Int -> Int = fun x -> x + 1
  let g : Num -> Num = f  // contravariant in argument

  // Compiled:
  let g : Num -> Num = fun x -> coerce_Int_to_Num(f(coerce_Num_to_Int(x)))
  // The function is eta-expanded with coercions at the boundaries
\end{verbatim}

