\subsection{Caching and Budgeting Recursive Specialization}

Specializing a call can change both its argument representation and
the selected dictionary. Reusing a clone based on function name alone
therefore risks calling the wrong implementation. In a hybrid compiler
with a callable generic entry, use a canonical key containing the
function, concrete types, and identities of any statically known
dictionaries substituted into the clone. Assume that the generic
entry can accept dynamic type and dictionary arguments and that the
compiler can build adapters in either direction. A single clone budget
limits the pass even if recursive calls keep requesting more types.

\begin{sudocode}
\Function{ChooseEntry}{$f,T,D$}
  \State $k\gets\Call{CanonicalKey}{f,T,D}$
  \If{the clone cache contains $k$}
    \State \Return cache entry for $k$ \Comment{Even if just declared}
  \EndIf
  \If{$k$ is ineligible or budget equals zero}
    \State \Return generic entry with adapters \Comment{Pass live evidence}
  \EndIf
  \State decrement budget
  \State $p\gets$ declare fresh specialized entry
  \State set cache entry for $k$ to $p$
  \State enqueue $(p,f,T,D)$ for body generation
  \State \Return $p$
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

For example, specialize \texttt{sum} on integers with the ordinary
addition dictionary and a budget of one. The first call declares
\texttt{sumInt} and decrements the budget to zero. While generating
that entry, an integer recursive call with the same dictionary
finds the reserved symbol despite the exhausted budget. A later
call with a saturating addition dictionary does not match that key
and takes the generic route with its own evidence. On inputs 120
and 20, ordinary 32-bit addition gives 140, while addition saturated
at 127 gives 127; sharing the clone would change the result.

The cache invariant is that each published key has a single entry
with the final calling convention, even while its body is pending.
The budget bounds the number of queued bodies, not their total
instruction count. A production pass can additionally limit estimated
code growth. Generic fallback is essential here; a compiler that
erases dictionaries and has no generic implementation cannot simply
stop at the budget and still emit a valid call.

\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:summary-51}

Type-directed compilation transforms typed source programs into efficient target code by exploiting type information. The choice between erasure, coercion, and type-passing determines the runtime representation, the overhead of polymorphism, and the opportunities for specialization. Dictionary passing compiles type classes to record passing; monomorphization eliminates polymorphism overhead at the cost of code size; and type-specialized code generation recovers primitive performance for generic code.

For further reading, we recommend the GHC compiler papers~\cite{peytonjones1993}, the study of type erasure by Cretin and R{\'e}my~\cite{cretin2014}, and the Rust compiler's approach to monomorphization.
